{"id":"db0f8653-7ae7-4e45-9ae1-169f07063515","object":"chat.completion","created":1785025943,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"calls must be a list\\\")\\n    accepted = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        call_id = call['call_id']\\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        parseable = call['parseable']\\n        if tokens <= 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"prediction_tokens must be positive\\\")\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        normalized_gain = gain / tokens\\n        is_accepted = parseable and (normalized_gain >= threshold)\\n        if is_accepted:\\n            accepted.append(call_id)\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            'accepted': is_accepted,\\n            'call_id': call_id,\\n            'normalized_gain': normalized_gain\\n        })\\n    return {\\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted,\\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that processes the payload. The payload contains a threshold and a list of calls. Each call has call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable. We need to compute gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call. Then normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens. If parseable AND normalized_gain >= threshold, then accepted. Return accepted_call_ids list and diagnostics list (in same order as calls) with fields accepted, call_id, normalized_gain. The normalized_gain must be a number (float). Output schema requires normalized_gain as number. Also note: \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" So we divide by prediction_tokens. But ensure prediction_tokens is positive? It seems from examples it's integer >0. But we can handle zero? Could raise ValueError. But spec says raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. We'll check.\n\nImplementation: iterate over calls, compute gain, normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens. Then check parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold. Build diagnostics list in order. accepted_call_ids = [call.call_id for call in calls if condition]. Output dict with accepted_call_ids and diagnostics.\n\nEdge cases: threshold can be 0.05. Use float comparison.\n\nReturn JSON object. The solve function returns dict.\n\nNow produce the implementation string. Need to escape for JSON.\n\nWrite the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # case is the payload directly\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be a list\")\n    accepted = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call['call_id']\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\n        parseable = call['parseable']\n        # Validate tokens >0?\n        if tokens <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"prediction_tokens must be positive\")\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        normalized_gain = gain / tokens\n        is_accepted = parseable and (normalized_gain >= threshold)\n        if is_accepted:\n            accepted.append(call_id)\n        diagnostics.append({\n            'accepted': is_accepted,\n            'call_id': call_id,\n            'normalized_gain': normalized_gain\n        })\n    return {\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted,\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\n    }\n\nCheck rounding? The spec says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". But output schema says normalized_gain is number, no rounding specified. The examples show many decimals. But maybe we should not round. The spec inside says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\"? It says \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" But then output schema says type number, not integer. Usually rounded to some precision? Not specified. Could be as computed. The fixture outputs? Not given. We'll keep as float without rounding.\n\nAlso note: \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\" Already done.\n\nNow need to produce JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a JSON string. Ensure proper escaping.\n\nWe'll write the code in a string, then encode.\n\nMake sure to use triple quotes? No, we need to escape newlines and quotes. Easiest: write multi-line string with \\n and escaped quotes.\n\nLet's produce:\n\n\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"calls must be a list\\\")\\n    accepted = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        call_id = call['call_id']\\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        parseable = call['parseable']\\n        if tokens <= 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"prediction_tokens must be positive\\\")\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        normalized_gain = gain / tokens\\n        is_accepted = parseable and (normalized_gain >= threshold)\\n        if is_accepted:\\n            accepted.append(call_id)\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            'accepted': is_accepted,\\n            'call_id': call_id,\\n            'normalized_gain': normalized_gain\\n        })\\n    return {\\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted,\\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\\n    }\"\n\nBut we need to ensure the string is valid JSON. So we must escape backslashes and quotes. The string above has single quotes. In JSON string, we need double quotes and escape backslashes. We'll convert to double quotes for dictionary keys? The code uses single quotes, which is fine in Python. But the JSON string should contain the Python source exactly. So we can keep single quotes. However, when embedding in JSON, we need to escape any double quotes that appear, and backslashes. There are no double quotes in the code except the ones in ValueError strings? Actually we used double quotes for the ValueError strings. So we need to escape those double quotes with backslash. Also newlines replaced with \\n. And backslashes? No backslashes in code except maybe in escape. We'll do:\n\n\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"calls must be a list\\\")\\n    accepted = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        call_id = call['call_id']\\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        parseable = call['parseable']\\n        if tokens <= 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"prediction_tokens must be positive\\\")\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        normalized_gain = gain / tokens\\n        is_accepted = parseable and (normalized_gain >= threshold)\\n        if is_accepted:\\n            accepted.append(call_id)\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            'accepted': is_accepted,\\n            'call_id': call_id,\\n            'normalized_gain': normalized_gain\\n        })\\n    return {\\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted,\\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\\n    }\"\n\nNow we need to escape the double quotes: \\\"calls must be a list\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"calls must be a list\\\\\\\"? Actually in JSON string, we need to precede double quote with backslash. So every double quote in the string must become \\\". So replace all \" with \\\". Also backslashes? None.\n\nWe'll do that. Also newlines become \\n.\